course: dictionary
lesson: 12946
title: Excellence
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Excellence is a noun. It names a quality: being extremely good, or being the best at something. You can see excellence in a piece of work, in a game, or in how someone behaves.
explain: Look at how the word is used. «The school is proud of its culture of excellence.» The words culture and proud tell you the school values very high standards. «She won an award for excellence in science.» An award is given for something very good, so excellence means very high quality.
explain: Excellence is uncountable, so you do not say "an excellence". You say «She showed excellence» or «The team played with excellence». To check your meaning, swap in "very high quality". If the sentence still makes sense, you have it right.
fact: Excellence means the quality of being extremely good, or being the best at something.
fact: Excellence is a noun, and it is uncountable: you cannot say "an excellence".
fact: «Excellent» is the adjective that goes with excellence, and «excel» is the verb: to be very good at something.
fact: Schools and sports use excellence in mottos and awards, such as «a centre of excellence».
fact: Excellence praises very high standards of work or behaviour.
exercise: «The class showed excellence in every test this term.» What does excellence mean here? | A short break from lessons | Being late every day | Being very quiet and calm | *Very high quality
exercise: «Ben won an award for excellence in sport.» What was the award given for? | Being late to training | Breaking a rule | Losing every match | *Being extremely good at sport
exercise: Which word is the adjective that goes with excellence? | excellence | excel | *excellent | exceed
test: «The team played with excellence and won every game.» What does this tell you about how they played? | They played only in the rain | They did not try at all | *They played to very high standards | They played carelessly and in a hurry
test: Is excellence countable? Which is correct? | Yes, you can say "three excellences" | It is an adjective | Yes, it is a verb | *No, it is uncountable, so you cannot say "an excellence"
exam: Which sentence uses excellence correctly? | She has three excellences in art. | She is more excellence than her brother. | She excellence the other painters. | *She showed excellence in her painting.
